<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lysimachus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lysimachus was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>, the successors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and later parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. He was defeated and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-corupedium">Battle of Corupedium</a> in 281 BCE. The victory brought much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lysimachus.jpg" alt="Lysimachus" width="279" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lisymachus,_marble_-_Ephesus_Museum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
